Step 5: Locating the verification function
Why we do this

We do not want to read all the code — it would take hours. Instead, we use the error messages as anchor points. We know the firmware prints “flag did not generate correctly” when the check fails. If we find where in the code this message is used, we have found the verification function.
Step 5a: find the string’s address in DROM

python3 -c "
target = b'flag did not generate correctly'
data = open('seg_0_0x3f400020.bin','rb').read()
load = 0x3f400020
idx = data.find(target)
print(f'String found at address: 0x{load + idx:08x}')
"

Why we add load + idx

idx is the position of the string in the file (local offset in the segment). But the code does not reference file offsets, it references memory addresses. Since segment 0 loads at 0x3f400020, the real address of the string is the load address plus the local offset. This is the address the code will use when it wants to print the message.

String found at address: 0x3f403680

Step 5b: find who references this address

Now we search the code segments for a reference to 0x3f403680. In code, when a function wants to use a string, it loads its address as a 4-byte constant. On Xtensa, which is little endian, the value 0x3f403680 is stored in memory with the bytes in reverse order: 80 36 40 3f.

python3 -c "
import os, struct
addrs = [0x3f403680, 0x3f4036a4, 0x3f4036f0]
code_segs = [f for f in os.listdir('.') if f.startswith('seg_') and
             any(x in f for x in ['40080000','400d0020','40084a14'])]
for addr in addrs:
    needle = struct.pack('<I', addr)   # '<I' = unsigned 32-bit little endian
    for seg in sorted(code_segs):
        data = open(seg,'rb').read()
        idx = data.find(needle)
        if idx != -1:
            load = int(seg.split('_')[2].replace('0x','').replace('.bin',''), 16)
            print(f'Ref to 0x{addr:08x} found in {seg} at address 0x{load+idx:08x}')
"

Why we also search the neighboring addresses (0x3f4036a4, 0x3f4036f0)

The three error messages (“flag did not generate”, “cloned hardware”, “buy the real hardware”) are logically related — they appear in the same verification function. Their addresses are close together in DROM (we saw them consecutive). If we find all three referenced in the same area of code, we have confirmation that we found the right function, not a coincidence.
What we get

Ref to 0x3f403680 found in seg_3_0x400d0020.bin at address 0x400d07c8
Ref to 0x3f4036a4 found in seg_3_0x400d0020.bin at address 0x400d07cc
Ref to 0x3f4036f0 found in seg_3_0x400d0020.bin at address 0x400d07d4

All three references are in segment 3 (the main code), very close to each other (0x400d07c8 - 0x400d07d4). We are inside the function that decides whether to generate the flag. Now we need to understand what it checks.
Step 6: Disassembly with objdump
Why we do this

Until now we worked with raw bytes. To understand the verification logic, we have to turn the bytes into readable instructions (disassembly). We need a disassembler that understands Xtensa.
Why objdump and not Ghidra

Ghidra (the most popular reverse engineering tool) has no native support for Xtensa — it needs a separate plugin that was not available for our version. Instead, the GCC toolchain for Xtensa includes an objdump that can disassemble Xtensa. The gcc-xtensa-lx106 package is for the ESP8266 (Xtensa LX106), not the ESP32 (LX6), but the disassembler recognizes enough of the base Xtensa instruction set for what we need. Some LX6-specific instructions will show up as unknown (excw), but the MAC verification part uses base instructions, so it reads cleanly.

sudo apt-get install -y gcc-xtensa-lx106

Disassemble the area of interest

First we locate the line we care about in the disassembled output:

xtensa-lx106-elf-objdump -m xtensa -b binary -D \
    --adjust-vma=0x400d0020 seg_3_0x400d0020.bin 2>/dev/null \
    | grep -n "400d77" | head -5

Why we use — adjust-vma

objdump treats the file as raw data (-b binary) and by default assumes it starts at address 0. But our segment loads at 0x400d0020. --adjust-vma=0x400d0020 tells objdump to number the addresses starting from the real address, so the displayed addresses match the ones we found in step 5. Without it, we would have to do manual conversions on every line.
