400d77b9:  820202    l8ui    a8, a2, 2       ; read byte 2
400d77bc:  808801    slli    a8, a8, 24
400d77bf:  a20203    l8ui    a10, a2, 3
400d77c2:  00aa11    slli    a10, a10, 16
400d77c5:  a08820    or      a8, a8, a10
400d77c8:  a20204    l8ui    a10, a2, 4
400d77cb:  80aa11    slli    a10, a10, 8
400d77ce:  a08820    or      a8, a8, a10
400d77d1:  a20205    l8ui    a10, a2, 5      ; read byte 5
400d77d4:  a08820    or      a8, a8, a10
400d77d7:  b09920    or      a9, a9, a11     ; a9 = bytes 0,1 combined (16 bits)
400d77da:  a19ae4    l32r    a10, (0x18fe)       ; load constant 0x18FE
400d77dd:  a79909    bne     a9, a10, 0x400d77ea ; if a9 != 0x18FE -> jump to error
400d77e0:  919ae4    l32r    a9, (0xcb957a39)    ; load constant
400d77e3:  9a88      add.n   a8, a8, a9          ; a8 = rest of MAC + 0xcb957a39
400d77e5:  3c19      movi.n  a9, 49
400d77e7:  87b919    bgeu    a9, a8, 0x400d7804  ; second check -> jump to error

What this code does, step by step

Register a2 points to a 6-byte buffer - this is the chip's MAC address (every ESP32 has a unique 6-byte MAC). The code:

    Reads the 6 MAC bytes one by one (l8ui = load 8-bit unsigned).
    Combines the first 2 bytes into a 16-bit value, in a9.
    Compares with 0x18FE (bne = branch if not equal). If the first 2 bytes of the MAC are not 0x18 0xFE, it jumps to the error code.
    Combines the last 4 bytes + a constant 0xcb957a39 and performs a second check (bgeu = branch if greater or equal unsigned).

Why 0x18FE matters

The first 3 bytes of any MAC address are the OUI (Organizationally Unique Identifier) — they identify the manufacturer. 0x18FE34 is one of the OUIs allocated to Espressif Systems. So the check says: "are you an authentic Espressif chip?". On a real ESP32 the first MAC bytes pass this check automatically. On a clone or in an emulator with a default MAC (00:00:00:...), the check fails, hence the "running on cloned hardware" message.

This is the anti-clone barrier. We have two options: give the emulator a valid Espressif MAC, or bypass the check directly in the code. We choose the bypass, because it is deterministic and does not depend on the emulator’s configuration.
Identify the exact bytes to patch

python3 -c "
data = open('seg_3_0x400d0020.bin','rb').read()
load = 0x400d0020
off1 = 0x400d77dd - load
off2 = 0x400d77e7 - load
print(f'bne  at 0x400d77dd (local offset 0x{off1:x}): {data[off1:off1+3].hex()}')
print(f'bgeu at 0x400d77e7 (local offset 0x{off2:x}): {data[off2:off2+3].hex()}')
"

bne  at 0x400d77dd (local offset 0x77bd): a79909
bgeu at 0x400d77e7 (local offset 0x77c7): 87b919

These are the 6 bytes (3+3) we will modify.
Step 7: Binary patch
Why we do this

We want the firmware to “believe” the hardware check passed, regardless of the real MAC. The cleanest method is to turn the two conditional branch instructions (bne, bgeu) into nop (no operation - does nothing). This way the code never jumps to the error message; it continues straight ahead toward flag generation.
Why nop is 0x000000

On Xtensa, the nop instruction (the 3-byte variant) is encoded as 0x000000. By replacing exactly the 3 bytes of each branch with zeros, we keep the instruction length (very important - if we changed the length, we would desync all the code that follows) and the effect becomes "do nothing".
Apply the patch directly in app.bin

python3 -c "
data = bytearray(open('app.bin','rb').read())
# Convert the virtual address into an offset in the app.bin file.
# Segment 3 has its header at file_offset 0x10018; the data starts after
# the 8-byte header, so at 0x10020. The segment's virtual address is 0x400d0020.
def vaddr_to_file(vaddr):
    return 0x10020 + (vaddr - 0x400d0020)
# Patch 1: bne -> nop
off1 = vaddr_to_file(0x400d77dd)
print(f'Patch1 offset 0x{off1:x}: {data[off1:off1+3].hex()} -> 000000')
data[off1:off1+3] = b'\x00\x00\x00'
# Patch 2: bgeu -> nop
off2 = vaddr_to_file(0x400d77e7)
print(f'Patch2 offset 0x{off2:x}: {data[off2:off2+3].hex()} -> 000000')
data[off2:off2+3] = b'\x00\x00\x00'
open('app_patched.bin','wb').write(data)
print('app_patched.bin saved')
"

Why the vaddr -> file conversion is necessary

Until now we worked with virtual addresses (how the code sees memory). But we apply the patch in the app.bin file, where the same data sits at a different offset. The formula 0x10020 + (vaddr - 0x400d0020) translates a virtual address in segment 3 into the corresponding position in the file. 0x10018 is where the segment header starts in the file (from step 4), and the +8 skips over the segment mini-header to reach the data.
Step 8: Recomputing the checksum and hash
Why we do this

Here is a subtle trap. Before running the application, the ESP32 bootloader verifies its integrity through two mechanisms: a checksum (simple XOR) and a SHA256 hash. These exist to detect corruption — but from their point of view, our patch is corruption. If we do not recompute them, the bootloader will refuse to start the application with an “image is corrupt” message, and we will fall into an infinite reboot loop.
Find the positions of the checksum and hash

python3 -c "
from esptool.bin_image import LoadFirmwareImage
img = LoadFirmwareImage('esp32', 'app.bin')
last = img.segments[-1]
end = last.file_offs + 8 + len(last.data)   # end of the last segment
end_aligned = (end + 15) & ~15              # align to 16 bytes
print(f'Checksum byte at offset: 0x{end_aligned-1:x}')
print(f'Hash starts at offset:   0x{end_aligned:x}')
print(f'Hash ends at:            0x{end_aligned+32:x}')
"

Why these positions

The ESP32 format places the checksum as the last byte of a 16-aligned region, right after the last segment. Immediately after comes the SHA256 hash (32 bytes). We compute the positions starting from the end of the last segment, align to 16, and from there we know exactly where each one is written.

Checksum byte at offset: 0x2290f
Hash starts at offset:   0x22910
Hash ends at:            0x22930

Recompute and write

python3 -c "
import hashlib
from esptool.bin_image import LoadFirmwareImage
data = bytearray(open('app_patched.bin','rb').read())
# Checksum = XOR of all bytes from all segments, starting from the value 0xEF.
# 0xEF is the starting value defined by the ESP32 format.
img = LoadFirmwareImage('esp32', 'app_patched.bin')
chk = 0xEF
for seg in img.segments:
    for b in seg.data:
        chk ^= b
data[0x2290f] = chk
print(f'New checksum: 0x{chk:02x}')
# SHA256 hash computed over the entire image content up to the hash position.
# The hash covers everything before it, so any change in the code changes it.
h = hashlib.sha256(bytes(data[:0x22910])).digest()
data[0x22910:0x22930] = h
print(f'New hash: {h.hex()}')
open('app_patched.bin','wb').write(data)
"

Verify that the bootloader will accept the image

