Checksum: 0x49 (valid)
Validation hash: ... (valid)

If both show up as valid, the image will pass the bootloader's checks. If not, something is wrong with the positions and there is no point going further.
Step 9: Rebuilding the complete flash image
Why we do this

We have the patched application, but the emulator does not run an isolated application — it runs a whole chip, which boots from the bootloader. The bootloader reads the partition table to know where the application is, then loads it. So we must rebuild a complete flash with all the pieces in their place: bootloader + partition table + patched application.

python3 -c "
firmware = open('firmware.bin','rb').read()
flash = bytearray(b'\xff' * 0x400000)  # 4MB of 'erased' flash (filled with 0xFF, like a new chip)
flash[0x1000:0x8000]   = firmware[0x1000:0x8000]   # bootloader (from the original)
flash[0x8000:0x10000]  = firmware[0x8000:0x10000]  # partition table (from the original)
app = open('app_patched.bin','rb').read()
flash[0x10000:0x10000+len(app)] = app              # the PATCHED application
open('flash_patched.bin','wb').write(flash)
print('flash_patched.bin ready')
"

Why we fill with 0xFF and take the bootloader/partitions from the original

We fill with 0xFF to simulate a real chip: unprogrammed regions are 0xFF. We copy the bootloader and partition table unchanged from the original firmware, because we did not modify them - only the application was patched. If we had skipped the partition table, the bootloader would not know where the application is and would fail with a "partition table error". Each piece at its standard offset.
Step 10: Emulation with QEMU Espressif
Why we do this

Now we actually execute the firmware. The hint from the challenge (“try to emulate it”) told us from the start that this was the way. Since we do not have a physical ESP32 (and we wanted to bypass the hardware check anyway), we emulate.
Why the Espressif QEMU and not the standard one

QEMU is the standard emulator for many architectures, but the version from the Linux packages does not include an esp32 machine - it emulates generic Xtensa, not the ESP32 chip with all its peripherals (flash controller, UART, etc.). Espressif maintains a QEMU fork with full support for the ESP32. That is the one we use.

cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/espressif/qemu/releases/download/esp-develop-9.0.0-20240606/qemu-xtensa-softmmu-esp_develop_9.0.0_20240606-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.xz -O qemu-esp32.tar.xz
tar xf qemu-esp32.tar.xz

Run

cd /path/to/espresso_fw/hw_espresso
/tmp/qemu/bin/qemu-system-xtensa -nographic -machine esp32 \
    -drive file=flash_patched.bin,if=mtd,format=raw 2>&1 | timeout 30 cat

What each option means

    -nographic: no graphical window; the output (the chip's UART) comes straight into the terminal. An ESP32 writes its logs to UART, so this is how we see what the firmware "says".
    -machine esp32: emulates the complete ESP32 chip (this is what the Espressif fork adds).
    -drive file=flash_patched.bin,if=mtd,format=raw: connects our flash as MTD memory (Memory Technology Device - the interface type for flash). format=raw means the file is exactly the flash content, with no wrapper.
    timeout 30 cat: we stop after 30 seconds, because the firmware runs forever; we only care about the output at startup.

What we get

I (12527) app_init: Project name:     espresso
...
I (12583) main_task: Calling app_main()
I (12583) main: HTB{xxxxxxxxx_xx_xx_xxxx!!!}
I (12583) main_task: Returned from app_main()

The firmware passed the (bypassed) hardware check, generated the flag, and wrote it to UART.
Flag

HTB{xxxxxxxxx_xx_xx_xxxx!!!} # FIND THE FLAG BY YOURSELF! 

The flag itself confirms the intent of the challenge: emulating the hardware was the solution.
Summary: the complete logical chain

Every step followed logically from the previous one:

    The file starts with 0xFF -> it is a flash dump, not an ordinary executable.
    Magic byte 0xE9 at 0x1000 and 0x10000 -> it is an ESP32 flash with the standard layout.
    The error messages -> the firmware only generates the flag on “real hardware”, and suggests emulation.
    The segments with their real addresses -> we can correctly analyze the code.
    The references to the error string -> we locate the verification function in segment 3.
    The disassembly -> the check is on the OUI 0x18FE from the MAC address (anti-clone).
    Patching nop over the two branches -> we bypass the check.
    Recomputing the checksum + hash -> the bootloader accepts the modified image.
    Rebuilding the complete flash -> the emulator has everything it needs to boot.
    QEMU Espressif -> the firmware runs, generates the flag, writes it to UART.

